SOURCE FACT. MCPStore.swift:63-71 verbatim:
  func value(for field: MCPField, of entry: MCPCatalogEntry) -> String {
      if let draft = drafts[entry.id]?[field.key] { return draft }
      if field.kind == .secret {
          if let secret = MCPKeychain.value(server: entry.id, field: field.key) { return secret }
      } else if let stored = connections[entry.id]?.values[field.key] {
          return stored
      }
      return field.defaultValue ?? ""
  }
It is if / else-if. A .secret field reads the Keychain ONLY and can never fall through to connections[id].values[key]; a non-secret field never touches the Keychain. Both kinds fall to field.defaultValue ?? "". Note the draft branch returns the draft even when it is the empty string (if-let, not a non-empty check).
The related-but-different helper storedValues(for:) (:293-301) IS a merge: it starts from connections[entry.id]?.values ?? [:] and overlays the Keychain value for every .secret field that has one. And storedValue(server:field:secret:) (:203-206) is a third rule: secret -> Keychain only, else connection only, no default.
MCPKeychain.value returns nil unconditionally when CaptureRun.isEnabled (MCPKeychain.swift:12: guard !CaptureRun.isEnabled else { return nil }) and also returns nil when the stored value trims to empty (:24).

